---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import { specification, url } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="How ANS works"
  description="Understand ANS registration, DNS discovery, transparency logs, and peer authentication through mTLS or DPoP."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="How it works"
    title="From a domain name to a verifiable peer."
    description="ANS combines domain control, certificates, and a transparency log. Each part answers a specific question about the agent you are about to interact with."
  />
  <section class="container content-section split-content">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The lifecycle</p>
      <h2>
        Register. Publish.
        <br />
        Discover. Verify.
      </h2>
      <p>
        The agent operator, Registration Authority, and Transparency Log have
        distinct roles. DNS helps peers find the evidence.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>A name for a specific registration</strong>
        <code>ans://v1.0.0.payments.example.com</code>
        <p>
          The version and domain identify the registration. A new version is a
          new registration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ol class="flow-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Prove control and register</h3>
        <p>
          The operator submits an agent host, version, endpoints, and
          certificate material to a Registration Authority. The RA verifies
          domain control and the required proofs.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Publish the required DNS records</h3>
        <p>
          The RA returns the records to publish. The operator provisions them
          with their DNS provider; the RA checks the required records before
          activation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Seal the lifecycle event</h3>
        <p>
          The RA signs the event and the Transparency Log seals it before
          activation succeeds. Public verification artifacts bind the
          registration to its authorized certificates.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Discover the endpoint and evidence</h3>
        <p>
          Peers use a supported discovery profile to locate endpoints. The
          shared <code>_ans-badge</code> record locates the registration’s badge
          in its Transparency Log.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Authenticate, then apply policy</h3>
        <p>
          Check identity, current status, and key possession. The application
          then applies its own permissions and risk policy before allowing an
          action.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section class="container content-section">
    <h2>Three proofs. One authenticated identity.</h2>
    <div class="three-columns principles">
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">01</span>
        <h3>Identity</h3>
        <p>
          Is this certificate bound to this agent in the transparency log? A
          badge or a verified SCITT receipt supplies the identity evidence.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">02</span>
        <h3>Current status</h3>
        <p>
          Is the registration still valid? An online badge or a short-lived
          signed status token supplies lifecycle information with bounded
          freshness.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">03</span>
        <h3>Possession</h3>
        <p>
          Does this peer hold the private key? Public badges and receipts alone
          cannot authenticate the caller. The peer must prove possession.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container content-section">
    <h2>Choose the proof that fits your connection.</h2>
    <p>
      ANS-6 defines two caller authentication methods with equal standing in the
      published specification. Both require identity and current-status checks.
    </p>
    <div class="two-columns" style="margin-top: 28px">
      <article class="feature-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">Method A</p>
        <h3>Mutual TLS</h3>
        <p>
          The caller presents its ANS Identity Certificate during the TLS
          handshake. This fits a connection where the peer’s TLS identity
          reaches the verifier.
        </p>
        <p>Can use online badge verification or SCITT artifacts.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-block">
        <p class="eyebrow">Method B</p>
        <h3>Application-layer DPoP</h3>
        <p>
          The caller sends a signed, single-use proof bound to the request
          method, URL, and content. It can pass through gateways that terminate
          TLS.
        </p>
        <p>Uses a SCITT receipt and status token, with replay protection.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="section-note">
      Callee verification also binds the actual TLS server certificate to the
      registration.{' '}
      <a href={`${specification}/blob/main/spec/ans-6-agent-authentication.md`}>
        Read the full ANS-6 verification procedures ↗
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container content-section">
    <h2>DNS is discovery, with verifiable bindings.</h2>
    <p>
      The current V2 registration model defaults to <code>ANS_DNSAID</code>.{' '}
      <code>ANS_TXT</code> is an explicit option. Publish the records returned
      by your RA, including their required flags.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top: 25px">
      <table>
        <caption class="eyebrow" style="text-align:left;margin-bottom:15px">
          Published discovery profiles
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Record</th>
            <th scope="col">Location</th>
            <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">ANS_DNSAID</th>
            <td>
              SVCB at <code>agentHost</code>
            </td>
            <td>Per-endpoint discovery hints. The default V2 profile.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">ANS_TXT</th>
            <td>
              <code>_ans.agentHost</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              TXT discovery records; opt-in on V2 and used by the V1 lane.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Badge</th>
            <td>
              <code>_ans-badge.agentHost</code>
            </td>
            <td>Required, shared link to the Transparency Log badge.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Server DANE</th>
            <td>
              <code>_port._tcp.agentHost</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              Optional TLSA record. A DNSSEC-authenticated mismatch must not be
              accepted.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="section-note">
      <a href={`${specification}/blob/main/spec/ans-3-dns-publication.md`}>
        DNS publication and profile details ↗
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container content-section split-content">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust boundaries</p>
      <h2>Evidence has a scope.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>
        ANS verifies domain-anchored agent identity. Optional Verified
        Identities can add a separately proven identity relationship where the
        RA supports them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sealed metadata digests can bind published metadata to a registration.
        They do not attest the code executing on a remote machine or guarantee
        an agent’s behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        Transparency makes records auditable. Clients still need trusted log
        keys, correct signature and proof verification, freshness checks, and a
        policy for failures.
      </p>
      <p>
        ANS authentication does not authorize an action or delegate a user’s
        permissions. Trust scoring is a separate project, not an ANS
        authentication tier.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" style="margin-top:25px" href={url('docs/')}>
        Read the specifications <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
